        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Types of Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/types-of-treadmills/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What are the main types of treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The main types are motorized treadmills, which come in folding and non-folding designs; manual treadmills, which are either flat or curved; walking pads and under-desk treadmills; incline trainers; and commercial treadmills. Smart, dog and VR treadmills are specialist kinds. The biggest split is whether a motor drives the belt or you do.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between a manual and a motorized treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A motorized treadmill uses an electric motor to drive the belt at a speed you set, usually with a powered incline. A manual treadmill has no motor: you move the belt with your legs. Flat manual models are cheap and basic walkers; curved manual models are expensive sprint and interval trainers. Manual machines need no outlet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a walking pad a type of treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. A walking pad is a compact motorized treadmill designed for walking, usually without a console upright or handrail, controlled by a remote or app, and low enough to slide under furniture. Decks and motors are smaller than on a full treadmill and many are not designed for running, so it suits walkers rather than runners.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
