        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the main types of treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The main types are motorized treadmills, which come in folding and non-folding designs; manual treadmills, which are either flat or curved; walking pads and under-desk treadmills; incline trainers; and commercial treadmills. Smart, dog and VR treadmills are specialist kinds. The biggest split is whether a motor drives the belt or you do.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a manual and a motorized treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A motorized treadmill uses an electric motor to drive the belt at a speed you set, usually with a powered incline. A manual treadmill has no motor: you move the belt with your legs. Flat manual models are cheap and basic walkers; curved manual models are expensive sprint and interval trainers. Manual machines need no outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walking pad a type of treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A walking pad is a compact motorized treadmill designed for walking, usually without a console upright or handrail, controlled by a remote or app, and low enough to slide under furniture. Decks and motors are smaller than on a full treadmill and many are not designed for running, so it suits walkers rather than runners.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of treadmill is best for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, a folding motorized treadmill with a powered incline is the most versatile choice. Regular runners, tall users and heavier users are usually better served by a non-folding machine with a longer deck and stronger motor. People who only walk and are short of space are often happiest with a walking pad.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a treadmill and an incline trainer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An incline trainer is a motorized treadmill with a much steeper incline range, typically up to around 40% against about 10 to 15% on most treadmills, and usually a decline function as well. It costs more, weighs more and rarely folds. It suits hikers and steep walkers; most other people never use the extra range.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are VR treadmills good for exercise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can be physically demanding, but they are designed for moving naturally inside virtual-reality games rather than for walking or running fitness. Most consumer models have no belt: you slide your feet on a low-friction base wearing special shoes, held by a harness or support arm. They do not replace a treadmill if fitness is the main goal.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Choosing a Type of Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most regrettable treadmill purchases are the wrong type rather than the wrong brand. These are the usual ones.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a walking pad to run on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most walking pads are built for walking, with short decks and small motors. If you intend to run, even occasionally, a full motorized treadmill is the right type.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a curved treadmill for easy walking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Curved manual treadmills shine at sprints and intervals, but slow, steady walking on one takes more effort than people expect. For daily walking, a motorized treadmill is easier to live with.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating smart as a type</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A large screen can sit on modest hardware. Choose the type and the running specification first, then decide whether you want connected features and the subscription that comes with them.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not measuring for the type</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Non-folding, commercial and curved machines are long and heavy, and even folded treadmills take more room than expected. Measure the space and the delivery route before choosing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

